  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n(Saturday - Sunday)\n\nRather pleasant conditions are expected over the weekend as a large \narea of surface high pressure across the Great Lakes will maintain a \nsteady stream of dry easterly low level flow. The most notable \naspect of this weekend's weather is expected to be a very slight \nwarming trend as the dry post-frontal airmass is slowly warmed by \npersistent insolation. This should yield temperatures in the mid to \nupper 70s by Sunday, which is slightly above the mathematical \naverage for this time of year but still very seasonable. Meanwhile, \ndewpoints in the upper 40s to low 50s, light winds, and mostly clear \nskies should continue to allow overnight temperatures to drop to \nsimilar values each morning. \n\n(Monday - Thursday)\n\nLate Sunday through Monday, an upper level trough will dig into the \nGreat Lakes and briefly reinforce northwesterly flow aloft, and will \nalso send another weak cold front through the area. This is expected \nto both act as a speed bump for our weekend warmup, and also further \nentrench our area within a dry, continental airmass for the \nforeseeable future. As such, temperatures Monday are likely to drop \nback to within a few degrees above average, with hardly a cloud in \nsight. In other words, excellent early fall conditions.\n\nFrom Tuesday onward, long-range ensemble guidance continues to \nsuggest that a rather stagnant pattern will develop across the \nCONUS, with a broad upper ridge across the western 2/3rds of the \ncountry that slowly expands eastward. As such, temperatures are \nfavored to slowly warm over the middle and latter portion of the \nweek, with relatively minimal spread among ensemble members. \nMeanwhile, the previously mentioned continental airmass is expected \nto linger across the area for several days and significantly limit \nprecipitation chances for the next week, with almost unanimous \nsupport for dry weather throughout the ensemble envelope.",
